WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.374
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:05.658 --> 00:00:07.200
SPEAKER: In Google
Kubernetes Engine,

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:09.540
our GKE's Cluster
Autoscaler feature

00:00:09.540 --> 00:00:12.240
enables clusters to
automatically resize

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:13.920
the number of nodes
in a node pool

00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:18.090
without manual intervention
based on your workload demands.

00:00:18.090 --> 00:00:21.000
The autoscaler triggers
the node scale-up events

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:24.870
when there are unscheduable pods
and node scale-down events when

00:00:24.870 --> 00:00:26.790
there are underutilized nodes.

00:00:26.790 --> 00:00:29.430
The autoscaler prints
any scaling decisions

00:00:29.430 --> 00:00:32.250
made to increase or decrease
the size of the node pool,

00:00:32.250 --> 00:00:35.360
any errors that occurred
during the scaling process, et

00:00:35.360 --> 00:00:38.970
cetera, as events or
entries in Cloud Logging.

00:00:38.970 --> 00:00:41.430
The Cluster Autoscaler
logs can help

00:00:41.430 --> 00:00:44.580
in investigating any
issues with autoscaling.

00:00:44.580 --> 00:00:46.470
In this video, we
will look at how

00:00:46.470 --> 00:00:48.990
to view the Cluster
Autoscaler logs, including

00:00:48.990 --> 00:00:51.840
how to troubleshoot and resolve
one of the events observed

00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:52.920
in the log.

00:00:52.920 --> 00:00:57.060
First, we will learn how to view
the Cluster Autoscaler logs.

00:00:57.060 --> 00:01:00.990
In the Google Cloud console,
go to the Logs Explorer page.

00:01:00.990 --> 00:01:03.090
From the Resources
dropdown list,

00:01:03.090 --> 00:01:07.620
select Kubernetes Cluster, then
select the location and name

00:01:07.620 --> 00:01:10.140
of your cluster.

00:01:10.140 --> 00:01:12.240
From the Log Name
dropdown list, select

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:17.760
container.googleapis.com/cluster
autoscaler visibility.

00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:21.300
From the Time Range dropdown
list, select a time range.

00:01:21.300 --> 00:01:24.900
In our cluster, we'll view
the logs for the last hour.

00:01:24.900 --> 00:01:27.120
Cluster Autoscaler
events may also

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:30.240
show up as notifications in
the Google Cloud Console user

00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:31.260
interface.

00:01:31.260 --> 00:01:33.270
Notifications can
help you monitor

00:01:33.270 --> 00:01:37.680
scaling events in the cluster
and act on them if needed.

00:01:37.680 --> 00:01:40.390
Oh, there's indeed a
notification in our cluster

00:01:40.390 --> 00:01:43.620
which states that the Cluster
Autoscaler can't scale-up nodes

00:01:43.620 --> 00:01:46.290
because node
auto-provisioning is disabled.

00:01:46.290 --> 00:01:48.570
The Details section
below the notification

00:01:48.570 --> 00:01:51.870
may have some additional
information about the event.

00:01:51.870 --> 00:01:55.620
If you click on Logs, it will
take you to the Logs Explorer

00:01:55.620 --> 00:01:57.030
page directly.

00:01:57.030 --> 00:01:59.790
Now, let us look at the node
scale-up event, which is one

00:01:59.790 --> 00:02:02.460
of the most common event types.

00:02:02.460 --> 00:02:05.880
A node scale-up event is logged
if the autoscaler cannot scale

00:02:05.880 --> 00:02:07.440
the cluster any further.

00:02:07.440 --> 00:02:09.960
This happens if there
are unscheduable pods

00:02:09.960 --> 00:02:12.660
in the cluster and the
autoscaler cannot scale

00:02:12.660 --> 00:02:15.090
the cluster up to
accommodate them.

00:02:15.090 --> 00:02:18.270
You can refer to the
fields, like reason

00:02:18.270 --> 00:02:23.790
and napFailureReason, to
understand why scale-up failed.

00:02:23.790 --> 00:02:26.130
The reason field
provides information

00:02:26.130 --> 00:02:28.410
on why the Cluster
Autoscaler is prevented

00:02:28.410 --> 00:02:30.330
from scaling up the nodes.

00:02:30.330 --> 00:02:35.100
For a cluster, the reason field
has the value "max node group

00:02:35.100 --> 00:02:36.330
size reached."

00:02:36.330 --> 00:02:39.690
This indicates that the Cluster
Autoscaler could not scale up

00:02:39.690 --> 00:02:42.480
because the maximum node
size for the node pool

00:02:42.480 --> 00:02:43.560
has been reached.

00:02:43.560 --> 00:02:46.440
Hence, no more
nodes can be added.

00:02:46.440 --> 00:02:49.560
The napFailureReason
field provides information

00:02:49.560 --> 00:02:52.860
on what prevented the Cluster
Autoscaler from provisioning

00:02:52.860 --> 00:02:54.870
additional node pools.

00:02:54.870 --> 00:02:57.990
For our cluster, this
field is populated

00:02:57.990 --> 00:03:00.970
with value
"no.scale.up.nap.disabled,"

00:03:00.970 --> 00:03:03.540
which means that node
auto-provisioning did not

00:03:03.540 --> 00:03:07.200
provision any new node groups
because node auto-provisioning

00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:09.000
is disabled.

00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:11.910
To solve this problem, we can
either increase the max size

00:03:11.910 --> 00:03:13.920
limit for autoscaling
for the node pool

00:03:13.920 --> 00:03:17.340
or enable node auto-provisioning
for the cluster.

00:03:17.340 --> 00:03:19.350
Check out the
documentation to see more

00:03:19.350 --> 00:03:21.540
about reviewing Cluster
Autoscaler events

00:03:21.540 --> 00:03:24.690
and understanding the
different reasons for the same.

00:03:24.690 --> 00:03:28.040
[MUSIC PLAYING]

